WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
created an ugly red candle across most positions.

2
00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:05.920
The scanning to execution workflow

3
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.800
Martin's daily routine demonstrates systematic consistency. His 30-minute morning preparation

4
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:18.160
in strong markets reduces to 15-20 minutes during corrections, reflecting reduced opportunity

5
00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.160
sets.

6
00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.160
These watch lists provide organisational structure.

7
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:29.320
The main watch list is for key stocks and immediate trade ideas.

8
00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:35.240
Leading stocks are names in clear uptrends, where the 9 EMA is above the 21 EMA and the

9
00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:38.560
21 above the 50.

10
00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:42.520
Mediocre stocks without clear directional bias.

11
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:51.100
lagging names in downtrends, and pillar, mega-cup stocks affecting indices.

12
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:56.440
The number of stocks in each category serves as a market health indicator. When the leading

13
00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:03.520
list contracts and lagging expands, it signals unfavorable conditions for breakout strategies.

14
00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:08.360
In market hours, he looks for breakout entries aligning with the precise entry criterion

15
00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.240
that offer very tight stops.

16
00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:17.720
Once he's in the stocks, he follows his well-laid-out exit rules.

17
00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.760
Martin's approach evolves with market conditions.

18
00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:25.920
During the late 2024 correction, he developed short-selling setups targeting stocks bouncing

19
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.480
into declining, converging EMAs.

20
00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:34.960
These inverse breakouts apply the same risk management principles but capitalise on downward

21
00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:37.280
momentum.

22
00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.420
His equity curve feedback principle proves crucial.

23
00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:45.560
If your trades work while indices struggle, continue with slight caution.

24
00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:50.040
If indices are strong but your trades fail, reassess aggressively.

25
00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:56.400
This prevents the common mistake of fighting your own performance data.

26
00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.840
Perhaps most importantly, Martin's journey illustrates the psychological evolution required

27
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:01.000
for trading success.
